# Security headers for the files Cloudflare serves WITHOUT invoking the Worker.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. `headers()` in next.config.js sets the full set
# on every response the Next server produces, which is every document on all
# three hosts. On Cloudflare that is not every response: Workers Static Assets
# answers /_next/static/** and everything from public/ at the edge, ahead of
# the Worker, so those responses never pass through Next and carried none of
# the headers. Measured on the preview before this file: HSTS present on the
# document, absent on /_next/static/chunks/*.js.
#
# WHY NOT ROUTE THE ASSETS THROUGH THE WORKER INSTEAD. `run_worker_first` can
# do it, and it is the wrong tool: every asset request would become a billed
# Worker invocation and lose its edge cache, which is exactly what the narrow
# two-path video rule in wrangler.jsonc is careful to avoid. See the Range
# requests section in CLAUDE.md. `_headers` is the mechanism Cloudflare
# provides for this and it costs nothing.
#
# WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CARRY. No Content-Security-Policy and no
# X-Frame-Options: both govern how a DOCUMENT behaves, and neither means
# anything on a script, a stylesheet or a JPEG. The two that do are here.
#
# THIS IS A SECOND PLACE HEADERS ARE SET. Keep it to the asset-relevant subset
# and keep the values identical to SECURITY_HEADERS in next.config.js; if you
# change one, change both. Cloudflare consumes this file and does not serve
# it. Vercel and GoDaddy ignore it entirely — their asset responses already
# come through the Next server, which applies headers() to them.

/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
